!.env.example
*.pfx
*.p12
appsettings.*.local.json

# A developer's own machine: a real server, a real login, a real password. It used to be tracked
# and was safe only because it used Trusted_Connection and named no password - the moment anyone
# pointed it at a SQL login, the password would have been committed. Copy the .example beside it
# and fill it in; that one is tracked and holds placeholders.
appsettings.Development.json

# Same reasoning for the compose override: it points the stack at a developer's own SQL Server and
# carries that login's password, so it is machine-specific and never committed (as in Weir). The
# tracked .example beside it holds placeholders - copy it and fill in your connection.
docker-compose.override.yml

# Tooling artifacts (ruflo / claude-flow). Local assistant configuration, not project source:
# it is regenerated per machine and carries no decision anyone reviews, so it stays out of the
# repository as it does in Flare and Weir.
ruvector.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
.claude/
.claude-flow/
.swarm/
.mcp.json

# OS
Thumbs.db
.DS_Store

# Machine-specific PWA config mount (Local auth testing)
pwa-appsettings.json
